      "program_name": "Napa County Housing Authority Farmworker Centers (CSA No. 4 assessment and HCD matching funds)",
      "administering_agency": "Napa County Housing Authority (Board of Supervisors sitting as the Authority)",
      "funding_type": "other",
      "typical_award_size": "Operating support for three county-owned dormitory centers (180 beds, about 400 farmworkers a year): County Service Area No. 4 vineyard assessment at $14 per planted acre raises about $650,000 a year (about 30% of operating cost); HCD Napa County Farmworker Centers Account match up to $250,000 a year (Health and Safety Code 50711.5, funded by SB 2); AB 1890 (Chapter 905, Statutes of 2026, signed Sept 30, 2026) raises the match to $500,000 a year once the Legislature appropriates it; AB 317 (2017) $200,000 General Fund support expires FY 2028-29. The County Affordable Housing Fund also subsidizes operations.",
      "per_unit_cap": null,
      "affordability_requirement": "Centers serve migrant and seasonal farmworkers; AB 1890 bars use of the state funds to house H-2A workers.",
      "eligible_project_types": [
        "farmworker housing"
      ],
      "application_cycle": "closed/inactive",
      "next_deadline": "Not available to outside developers; funds flow only to the County-owned centers. CSA 4 assessment is reauthorized by growers every five years.",
      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "true",
      "layering_notes": "Negative finding for private sponsors: Napa's only county housing authority is a farmworker-center operator with no Housing Choice Vouchers or development lending; the public housing agency for the county is the Housing Authority of the City of Napa. Farmworker rental projects in Napa instead layer the state Joe Serna Jr. Farmworker Housing Grant, the State Farmworker Housing Tax Credit (Pass 1) and the County Affordable Housing Fund, which once reserved $2,000,000 for farmworker projects and whose fees exempt farmworker units of 1,200 sq ft or less. The 2024 Napa County Farmworker Housing Needs and Impacts Assessment found 16% of year-round and 26% of seasonal workers in overcrowded housing. napacounty.gov pages and DocumentCenter PDFs sit behind Cloudflare and refused automated access on 2026-11-06, so the county's own page could not be opened; the Authority page is https://www.napacounty.gov/467/Housing-Authority (707-253-4112).",
      "source_url": "https://california.public.law/codes/health_and_safety_code_section_50711.5",
